        "reference": "RE-LEVIED #962. Originally `-> malloc`, with the note 'malloc family = C-specific allocation semantic (calls + instantiates pair is intentional: call graph fact + allocation)'. Two measured findings amend the TARGET. (1) FAMILY: across all 25 M12 truths that pin an `instantiates` edge the target is always a TYPE -- `Point`, `HashMap`, `List`, `Dictionary`, `User`, `UserService`, `MemoryStorage`, `Shape`. C's `-> malloc` was the ONLY non-type target in the family and can never resolve to a type symbol. (2) THE CAST STATES THE TYPE: Module.c:26 is `Buffer *buf = (Buffer *)malloc(sizeof(Buffer));` -- the allocation produces a `Buffer` and the code says so. The analyzer now publishes `instantiates <fn> -> <the cast or sizeof type>` and requires that type to be declared in the SAME FILE, because an `instantiates` edge must resolve to a type symbol. (3) WHY IT IS STILL UNMET: `Buffer` is declared in Module.H, so from Module.c it is cross-file and C-D2 forbids resolving it -- the same limit this truth already records for cross-file declarations. The row is therefore RETARGETED to the type the source names and recorded `unmetUnder: C-D2` rather than deleted: the semantic is wanted and now implemented, and it fires at corpus scale (2 edges in libgit2) wherever a typed allocation IS resolvable in-file. An allocation into `void *` with no cast publishes no edge, which is the honest outcome -- there is no type to instantiate."
      }
    },
